1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
This video is going to
be all about debugging

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:04,890
your driver inside
the Linux kernel.

3
00:00:04,890 --> 00:00:07,110
The objectives for today,

4
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:08,970
we're going to talk about
strategies for debug of

5
00:00:08,970 --> 00:00:11,670
kernel code and specifically
your driver code.

6
00:00:11,670 --> 00:00:14,115
We're going to talk about
strategies for using printk

7
00:00:14,115 --> 00:00:17,890
and handling of, oops messages.

8
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:24,300
The book talks a
lot in Chapter 4

9
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,910
about why kernel debugging

10
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:28,830
is harder than maybe what you've

11
00:00:28,830 --> 00:00:31,245
experienced so far with
user-space debugging.

12
00:00:31,245 --> 00:00:33,795
First of all, you
might be used to using

13
00:00:33,795 --> 00:00:36,465
debuggers like GDB
with user-space tools.

14
00:00:36,465 --> 00:00:39,770
While it's still possible
to analyze with GDB,

15
00:00:39,770 --> 00:00:42,410
the book talks about
using /proc/kcore.

16
00:00:42,410 --> 00:00:45,080
You can't do a lot of the
things that make GDB useful.

17
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:46,430
You can't really halt, set,

18
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:50,115
breakpoints, modify
memory for instance.

19
00:00:50,115 --> 00:00:51,680
There are other ways with KGDB

20
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,165
that you can do
some of this stuff.

21
00:00:53,165 --> 00:00:55,340
But in general,
it's not really the

22
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:57,950
typical preferred
go-to method to debug,

23
00:00:57,950 --> 00:00:59,840
It's not something you're
going to typically start

24
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:02,315
with for debugging your
kernel source code.

25
00:01:02,315 --> 00:01:05,630
It's also not really
easy to trace.

26
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:08,645
There are tools that
will trace for us,

27
00:01:08,645 --> 00:01:10,550
but there are certain amount
of setup that we need to

28
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:12,910
do and a little bit of overhead.

29
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:15,380
In terms of getting
all that setup.

30
00:01:15,830 --> 00:01:18,875
It can be quite difficult
to reproduce bugs,

31
00:01:18,875 --> 00:01:21,425
especially ones that
are timing-related.

32
00:01:21,425 --> 00:01:23,960
The best strategy this

33
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,300
is to really think about
the design ahead of time

34
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:29,150
and not make sure
that your design

35
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:30,470
is such that you
won't have to try to

36
00:01:30,470 --> 00:01:32,970
find these timing bugs later.

37
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:38,175
Finally, often bugs are
going to crash the system.

38
00:01:38,175 --> 00:01:40,430
Because we don't really have
the security that we have

39
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:42,500
with user-space
where we can take

40
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:45,320
a segmentation fault and
the kernel will connect to

41
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:47,120
collect a nice
package for us that

42
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:49,225
we can use to analyze with GDB.

43
00:01:49,225 --> 00:01:51,530
A lot of times bugs
that happened in

44
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:54,320
the kernel are going to
crash the system completely.

45
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,840
We can't really rely on
any cleanup mechanisms

46
00:01:56,840 --> 00:01:58,130
because we might be crashing

47
00:01:58,130 --> 00:01:59,815
something that's
related to clean up.

48
00:01:59,815 --> 00:02:02,840
A lot of times bugs
when they crash,

49
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:04,370
they might destroy
evidence that would be

50
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:07,040
helpful to use to figure
out what the crash was.

51
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:09,290
In general, the
takeaway here is that

52
00:02:09,290 --> 00:02:12,920
the stakes in terms of

53
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:15,110
having kernel bugs or

54
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:17,255
in terms of having
programming bugs,

55
00:02:17,255 --> 00:02:20,405
go up a level as we start
working in the kernel.

56
00:02:20,405 --> 00:02:23,135
You need to be more
diligent, more patient,

57
00:02:23,135 --> 00:02:26,915
more cognizant of anything
that might end up

58
00:02:26,915 --> 00:02:32,195
turning in some bug that could
cause any of these issues.

59
00:02:32,195 --> 00:02:34,360
How do we debug the kernel,

60
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:37,045
given all those restrictions
and limitations?

61
00:02:37,045 --> 00:02:40,845
There are a lot of
debugging in terms of,

62
00:02:40,845 --> 00:02:44,815
this is specifically talking
about how you could use

63
00:02:44,815 --> 00:02:50,420
debugging with
both GDB and KGDB.

64
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:54,190
There's a bunch of options
that are listed in the book in

65
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:55,750
terms of configs that
you can turn on and

66
00:02:55,750 --> 00:02:58,150
the kernel to help with this.

67
00:02:58,150 --> 00:03:00,040
Then there's also
a kernel hacking

68
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:01,780
section of menu
config that'll talk

69
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:05,840
about how you can do
debugging like GDB,

70
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:07,510
KGDB, and how you can get

71
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:09,100
additional information like lock

72
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:10,465
debugging out of the kernel.

73
00:03:10,465 --> 00:03:12,400
There's also SYSRQ that we'll

74
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:15,665
discuss later as a
strategy you can use here.

75
00:03:15,665 --> 00:03:19,715
But by far the most common
debug method that I've seen

76
00:03:19,715 --> 00:03:21,320
for debugging your device driver

77
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:23,885
generally is to use prints.

78
00:03:23,885 --> 00:03:28,450
Printk is really the
mechanism for doing this.

79
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:31,950
Essentially the equivalent
of printf from user-space.

80
00:03:31,950 --> 00:03:33,420
But of course, we're
not going to be able to

81
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:34,970
use printf because that's

82
00:03:34,970 --> 00:03:37,160
[inaudible] and we
don't have access to

83
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:39,985
those user-space libraries
from kernel space.

84
00:03:39,985 --> 00:03:42,185
One thing to notice here
is that there's no comma.

85
00:03:42,185 --> 00:03:44,330
This is just a
macro that's going

86
00:03:44,330 --> 00:03:46,460
to expand the
something that tells

87
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:49,205
the printk function
that this is a warning

88
00:03:49,205 --> 00:03:53,260
or the level of the
kernel message.

89
00:03:53,260 --> 00:03:56,190
The different levels that
are supported are Emergency,

90
00:03:56,190 --> 00:03:57,210
Alert, CRIT, ERROR,

91
00:03:57,210 --> 00:03:59,010
Warning, Notice, Info, Debug.

92
00:03:59,010 --> 00:04:01,775
These are in different
levels from 0-7.

93
00:04:01,775 --> 00:04:03,650
You can specify thresholds

94
00:04:03,650 --> 00:04:05,480
that tell where the
log messages should

95
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:07,100
go based on which level you've

96
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:09,545
selected for are some
different mechanisms.

97
00:04:09,545 --> 00:04:11,905
We'll talk about in a bit.

98
00:04:11,905 --> 00:04:15,390
Klogd and Syslogd
Daemons are example,

99
