254
00:10:39,695 --> 00:10:42,680
a production to scenario
or maybe something

255
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:45,035
useful to use if you
wanted to focus on

256
00:10:45,035 --> 00:10:46,280
a kernel driver as a part of

257
00:10:46,280 --> 00:10:49,100
your file project
implementation.

258
00:10:49,170 --> 00:10:51,880
You can also, another
thing the book

259
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:54,220
mentions is printk_rate limit.

260
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:57,655
There's a very similar
function, printk_ratelimited,

261
00:10:57,655 --> 00:11:00,955
that replaces the mentioned
in Device Drivers Book.

262
00:11:00,955 --> 00:11:03,520
But this just keeps you from
flooding the kernel logs.

263
00:11:03,520 --> 00:11:08,845
If you have a print that
might happen quite often,

264
00:11:08,845 --> 00:11:10,900
then you can use
printk_ratelimited

265
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:12,880
to control how often
that print actually

266
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:14,080
shows up in a log so you don't

267
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:15,520
end up with a log that just

268
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:19,400
has hundreds of the same
message over and over again.

269
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:23,290
In terms of debugging,

270
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:27,625
one concern we're going to
have and unfortunately,

271
00:11:27,625 --> 00:11:29,590
you'll probably see one
or more of these in

272
00:11:29,590 --> 00:11:32,635
your implementations
for the class.

273
00:11:32,635 --> 00:11:34,570
It's a fact of life
that you'll make

274
00:11:34,570 --> 00:11:37,525
a mistake and this was going
to result in a system fault.

275
00:11:37,525 --> 00:11:39,160
System fault is going
to happen due to

276
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:40,720
a bug in a specific driver,

277
00:11:40,720 --> 00:11:43,735
most likely yours if you're
doing driver development

278
00:11:43,735 --> 00:11:46,120
and it could result in a panic

279
00:11:46,120 --> 00:11:48,415
where the kernel
stops executing.

280
00:11:48,415 --> 00:11:51,565
It might leave the system in
a generally unusable state.

281
00:11:51,565 --> 00:11:52,990
By unusable state, I mean,

282
00:11:52,990 --> 00:11:54,295
it might not be enough to just

283
00:11:54,295 --> 00:11:55,945
unload and reload your driver.

284
00:11:55,945 --> 00:11:57,190
You need to be skeptical

285
00:11:57,190 --> 00:11:58,885
whenever you see a
system fault that

286
00:11:58,885 --> 00:12:00,370
you might need to
reboot the system

287
00:12:00,370 --> 00:12:02,080
or if you're using
a virtual machine,

288
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:03,250
you might need to reboot your

289
00:12:03,250 --> 00:12:05,900
virtual machine in
order to recover.

290
00:12:05,910 --> 00:12:09,820
One of the system faults that
are pretty common to see,

291
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:12,100
and again, you'll
probably, unfortunately,

292
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:14,470
be seeing one or more
of these as a part of

293
00:12:14,470 --> 00:12:17,890
your work in this course,
are oops messages.

294
00:12:17,890 --> 00:12:20,650
An example of an oops is

295
00:12:20,650 --> 00:12:23,755
a null pointer dereference of
an incorrect pointer value.

296
00:12:23,755 --> 00:12:25,480
Similar to what would cause

297
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:27,640
a segmentation fault
in user space.

298
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:30,445
This is also known
as a page fault.

299
00:12:30,445 --> 00:12:32,860
Again, this might
leave the system in

300
00:12:32,860 --> 00:12:34,750
an unusable state and
you might need to

301
00:12:34,750 --> 00:12:37,820
reboot the system
after you see this.

302
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:41,020
There's actually an
example provided in

303
00:12:41,020 --> 00:12:42,475
the LDD 3 examples that

304
00:12:42,475 --> 00:12:44,560
purposely writes
to a null pointer,

305
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:45,595
it's called faulty write.

306
00:12:45,595 --> 00:12:47,500
Just to show you an
example of how to deal

307
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:49,690
with an oops message
and give you

308
00:12:49,690 --> 00:12:53,440
practice parsing these so
that you can use the output

309
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:55,420
for yourself when you have these

310
00:12:55,420 --> 00:12:58,345
happen during your own
driver development.

311
00:12:58,345 --> 00:12:59,830
The first thing we notice about

312
00:12:59,830 --> 00:13:01,510
an oops message
is this unable to

313
00:13:01,510 --> 00:13:03,310
handle kernel null
pointer dereference

314
00:13:03,310 --> 00:13:04,690
at virtual address zero.

315
00:13:04,690 --> 00:13:06,325
This is already
giving us a hint,

316
00:13:06,325 --> 00:13:08,050
virtual address zero that looks

317
00:13:08,050 --> 00:13:11,000
like it's probably a
null pointer reference.

318
00:13:11,190 --> 00:13:13,390
The first line is
going to tell us what

319
00:13:13,390 --> 00:13:15,550
happened and then EIP.

320
00:13:15,550 --> 00:13:19,809
These register sections are
specific to, in our case X86,

321
00:13:19,809 --> 00:13:21,640
since I ran this on X86,

322
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:22,930
and so this is referencing

323
00:13:22,930 --> 00:13:25,835
the instruction pointer
reference for X86.

324
00:13:25,835 --> 00:13:28,200
But it's essentially telling us

325
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:30,930
where in code the
fault occurred.

326
00:13:30,930 --> 00:13:33,870
What this is saying is that
it happened four bytes in

327
00:13:33,870 --> 00:13:37,505
to a function that's
16 or X10 bytes long.

328
00:13:37,505 --> 00:13:42,280
The module that it was loaded
from was called faulty.

329
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:45,640
You can see the call
trace here in terms

330
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:49,220
of where the faulty
write occurred.

331
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:54,370
If you map this to this
really simple implementation,

332
00:13:54,370 --> 00:13:56,680
you can see that indeed

333
00:13:56,680 --> 00:13:59,425
shortly into the
faulty write function,

334
00:13:59,425 --> 00:14:01,750
we attempt to write
a null location.

335
00:14:01,750 --> 00:14:05,720
This would cause the oops
message that we're seeing here.

336
00:14:06,450 --> 00:14:09,430
One other useful tool
that you can use

337
00:14:09,430 --> 00:14:11,635
when you're trying to understand

338
00:14:11,635 --> 00:14:14,920
oops messages is
objdump or objdump

339
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:16,975
that's going to show you
the assembly content

340
00:14:16,975 --> 00:14:18,700
associated with an object file.

341
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:21,400
In this case, our object
file is going to be our.ko

342
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:25,255
file that we created from
the kernel compile process.

343
00:14:25,255 --> 00:14:30,009
Objdump-S is going to intermix
source with assembly,

344
00:14:30,009 --> 00:14:32,080
assuming that you have
kernel debug turned

345
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:34,345
on for your module builds

346
00:14:34,345 --> 00:14:36,610
and you'll see there's a
debug option in the make file

347
00:14:36,610 --> 00:14:39,830
that you can turn on
to get this option.

348
00:14:42,090 --> 00:14:45,055
If you're building for
buildroot for instance,

349
00:14:45,055 --> 00:14:48,520
and you want to get the
cross object dump output,

350
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:50,650
remember you'll need
something that can parse

351
00:14:50,650 --> 00:14:53,785
the output for whatever target
architecture you're using.

352
00:14:53,785 --> 00:14:55,540
In the buildroot case,
you'd look under

353
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:58,465
buildroot output host
bin aarc64-linux

354
00:14:58,465 --> 00:15:01,780
objdump to find the cross
object dump utility

355
00:15:01,780 --> 00:15:03,535
that I could run on X86,

356
00:15:03,535 --> 00:15:05,200
but would analyze a.ko

357
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:09,220
file built for a [inaudible]
platform for me.

358
00:15:09,220 --> 00:15:11,230
You can do something like this,

359
00:15:11,230 --> 00:15:13,555
the path to whatever the
object dump file is,

360
00:15:13,555 --> 00:15:15,040
dash S, and then the path to

361
00:15:15,040 --> 00:15:16,825
the module file that you build.

362
00:15:16,825 --> 00:15:18,790
You should see an output
that looks something like

363
00:15:18,790 --> 00:15:20,800
this where you
have, in this case,

364
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:22,210
I don't have source

365
00:15:22,210 --> 00:15:24,775
intermixed and it's
just showing assembly.

366
00:15:24,775 --> 00:15:27,940
But I can see that the
faulty write function

367
00:15:27,940 --> 00:15:29,950
is referenced here and I can see

368
00:15:29,950 --> 00:15:32,110
a reference here where I

369
00:15:32,110 --> 00:15:35,110
move a zero into X1 to
this next line here,

370
00:15:35,110 --> 00:15:39,850
the store of the value

371
00:15:39,850 --> 00:15:42,535
into the X1 register that we
previously set with zero.

372
00:15:42,535 --> 00:15:45,560
It's going to call it cause
at null pointer exception.